      "content": "Right, i get that. but i'm worried the students will get stuck in a loop of just calling everything \"problematic\" instead of actually reading the text. i was thinking of telling them that a writer like Conrad didn't necessarily intend to be hateful, but was just using the vocabulary he had to convey the gravity of the situation. like, if he used more \"correct\" or modern language, the impact of the colonial setting might actually be lost on the reader. wouldn't it be more accurate to say that the language is essentially a neutral tool for characterization and setting the scene, rather than a deliberate attempt to push a racist agenda? i want to defend the literary merit of the work without it sounding like i'm excusing the views.",
