      "content": "It is more accurate to posit that the language functions as a reflection of the *social reality* the author was observing, which includes the prevailing prejudices of the time. Rather than labeling the language as a neutral tool, frame it as reflecting the accepted linguistic conventions of the period that define the characters' world. This allows students to engage with the realistic texture of the novel without necessarily excusing its ideological underpinnings. The goal should be to analyze *how* the language functions to create that atmosphere, not to defend its moral content.",
